WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:03.321
Follow Langfocus on Facebook,
Twitter and Instagram.

00:00:15.481 --> 00:00:16.495
Hello everyone.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.322
Welcome to the Langfocus channel
and my name is Paul.

00:00:19.362 --> 00:00:21.925
Today's topic is the Indonesian language,

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:25.020
or Bahasa Indonesia as
it's called in Indonesian.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.620
Indonesian is closely connected with Malay.

00:00:27.645 --> 00:00:30.237
So, if you haven't seen my video
on the Malay language yet,

00:00:30.262 --> 00:00:33.301
I recommend that you watch that first
before you watch this video.

00:00:33.326 --> 00:00:35.040
Indonesian is based on Malay,

00:00:35.065 --> 00:00:38.320
though it has evolved and taken on
some uniquely Indonesian features.

00:00:38.437 --> 00:00:41.503
There are about 43 million
native speakers of Indonesian

00:00:41.528 --> 00:00:45.300
and about 155 million second
language speakers of Indonesian,

00:00:45.325 --> 00:00:49.180
mainly in Indonesia where it's the national
language and the sole official language.

00:00:49.205 --> 00:00:53.099
Why does Indonesian have so many more second
language speakers than native speakers?

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.782
Well, there are hundreds of
languages spoken in Indonesia

00:00:55.807 --> 00:00:58.107
and Indonesian functions as a lingua franca

00:00:58.132 --> 00:01:00.080
between speakers of different languages.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.099
It's the language of education and of media
and these days everybody learns Indonesian.

00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:08.100
But, most Indonesians still speak a different 
regional language as their native language

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.100
plus, Indonesian, the national language.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.039
How was the Indonesian language born?

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.247
Indonesian is based on the Malay language

00:01:14.272 --> 00:01:16.930
which has been a lingua franca
of trade and of the elite

00:01:16.954 --> 00:01:19.220
throughout the Malay
Archipelago for centuries.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.179
The story of Indonesian begins
during the colonial period.

00:01:22.204 --> 00:01:23.863
In the early 17th century,

00:01:23.888 --> 00:01:28.504
the VOC or the Dutch East India company
established a trading post at Batavia,

00:01:28.529 --> 00:01:30.220
the site of present-day Jakarta,

00:01:30.253 --> 00:01:32.460
and over the following
three centuries expanded

00:01:32.485 --> 00:01:34.586
into other areas of
present-day Indonesia.

00:01:34.611 --> 00:01:36.571
The Dutch East India company made Malay

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:38.866
the administrative language
of its trading outpost.

00:01:39.024 --> 00:01:42.243
In 1799, the Dutch East
India company went bankrupt

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:44.595
and the Dutch government
took control of the colony.

00:01:45.228 --> 00:01:46.514
In the late 18th century,

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:49.548
the British East India Company had
 also arrived in the archipelago.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.775
And in 1824, a treaty between
the British and the Dutch

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.560
divided the area between the British to
the north and the Dutch to the south.

00:01:56.585 --> 00:01:57.636
In the Dutch colony,

00:01:57.661 --> 00:02:00.771
the promotion of the Dutch language
 and education in Dutch began,

00:02:00.796 --> 00:02:03.679
but only to a limited extent,
mostly for the elite.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.403
Compared to other colonial powers,

00:02:05.428 --> 00:02:09.220
the Dutch were not really interested in changing
the culture of the colonized people.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:11.560
Their main goal was to facilitate trade.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:15.480
Therefore since Malay was already a lingua
franca that could facilitate trade,

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.760
there was no reason for them to
introduce Dutch as a lingua franca.

00:02:18.785 --> 00:02:21.415
The Dutch promoted Malay as
the language of education,

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:22.995
recognizing "Riau Malay",

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.240
the classical Malay spoken in the
Malacca and Johor Sultanates,

00:02:26.265 --> 00:02:27.595
as the standard language.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.969
The standard form of Malay
continued to develop

00:02:29.994 --> 00:02:32.700
with the abundance of Malay
literature in the 19th century.

00:02:32.848 --> 00:02:36.554
And in the 20th century, Malay linguists
published dictionaries and grammars

00:02:36.579 --> 00:02:38.758
to further standardize and
modernize the language.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.540
The Dutch language never became
 widespread in Indonesia.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:45.260
In fact, in 1940, only 2% of
Indonesians spoke Dutch .

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:48.146
But, even though the role of the
Dutch language was very limited,

00:02:48.171 --> 00:02:51.106
a large number of Dutch loanwords
 entered the Malay language

00:02:51.131 --> 00:02:52.780
in the areas under Dutch control.

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:56.072
And this is one of the things that
made the Indonesian variety of Malay

00:02:56.097 --> 00:02:57.486
different from other varieties.

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:00.844
In the early 20th century,
a pro-independence movement arose

00:03:00.869 --> 00:03:02.519
and people throughout the Dutch colony

00:03:02.544 --> 00:03:05.820
began to see themselves as a
single people, Indonesians.

00:03:05.948 --> 00:03:08.590
The term Indonesia was a new
term for the archipelago,

00:03:08.615 --> 00:03:10.534
created by European ethnologists

00:03:10.559 --> 00:03:12.880
but adopted by nationalists
in the Dutch colony.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:14.896
At the Youth Conference of 1928,

00:03:14.921 --> 00:03:18.499
young nationalists declared the
Youth Pledge with three ideals:

00:03:18.524 --> 00:03:21.500
one motherland, one nation,
and one language.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.831
The language they chose was the
 Indonesian variety of Malay,

00:03:24.856 --> 00:03:26.920
which they named "Bahasa Indonesia".

00:03:26.981 --> 00:03:29.845
When Indonesia declared
independence in 1945,

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:32.940
Indonesian was declared the national
language of the new country.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:34.180
But why Indonesian?

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:35.553
At the time of independence,

00:03:35.578 --> 00:03:38.910
Indonesian was the native language
of only 5% of the population,

00:03:38.935 --> 00:03:42.871
while Javanese was the native language
of over 40% of the population.

00:03:42.896 --> 00:03:46.060
So, why wasn't Javanese chosen
to be the national language?

00:03:46.163 --> 00:03:49.296
Well, that would have shown favoritism
to Indonesia's largest ethnic group,

00:03:49.321 --> 00:03:51.762
undermining the national
unity of the country.

00:03:51.816 --> 00:03:54.694
But Indonesian, previously
known just as Malay,

00:03:54.719 --> 00:03:57.755
had been a lingua franca throughout
the archipelago for centuries,

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.020
so people in various regions of the
country already knew some Malay.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:03.448
It was also a much simpler
language than Javanese

00:04:03.473 --> 00:04:06.980
and could realistically be learned by
millions of people as a second language.

00:04:07.005 --> 00:04:08.925
Indonesian turned out to
be the right choice,

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:11.893
successfully becoming the language
of government, education,

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:15.267
trade and communication between people
in different regions of the country.

00:04:15.378 --> 00:04:18.678
Indonesian completely replaced the
former colonial language Dutch

00:04:18.703 --> 00:04:20.035
as the language of public life,

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:22.440
that's something that's very rare 
in former colonies.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:25.978
Today, Indonesian is still the second
language of most Indonesian people,

00:04:26.003 --> 00:04:29.176
but it's becoming increasingly
more common as a native language,

00:04:29.201 --> 00:04:30.320
especially in the cities.

00:04:30.472 --> 00:04:33.558
That's because people from all over
the country moved to the big cities,

00:04:33.583 --> 00:04:35.963
so rather than speak their
native regional languages,

00:04:35.988 --> 00:04:37.580
they speak Indonesian instead.

00:04:37.679 --> 00:04:39.369
Some of them get married with each other,

00:04:39.394 --> 00:04:42.905
and they raise their children speaking
Indonesian as their primary language.

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:44.460
So, what is Indonesian like?

00:04:44.528 --> 00:04:47.178
Well, undoubtedly, one of the
things that made Indonesian

00:04:47.203 --> 00:04:51.000
so successful as a national language
so quickly was its simplicity.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.797
If you've seen my video on Malay,

00:04:52.822 --> 00:04:55.520
then you're already familiar with
the simplicity of Indonesian,

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.792
because in its standard form,
it's very close to standard Malay,

00:04:58.817 --> 00:05:00.560
just with some different vocabulary.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:02.220
Here are some features of Indonesian.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:04.855
Indonesian has simple
phonology and orthography.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.281
In general, one letter
represents just one sound.

00:05:07.306 --> 00:05:09.871
Indonesian has simple
SVO sentence structure.

00:05:09.896 --> 00:05:12.022
There are no case inflections 
in Indonesian.

00:05:12.094 --> 00:05:14.487
There's also no grammatical
gender in Indonesian.

00:05:14.512 --> 00:05:18.800
There's no plural form in Indonesian, and
explicit plurals are made by republication.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:21.400
Reduplication meaning that
you repeat the word again.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:24.680
And perhaps best of all:
no verb conjugations.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:29.000
Let's look at a few Indonesian sentences
and we'll identify some of those features.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.680
This sentence means :
"They are cooking eggs for breakfast"

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:38.960
Word-for-word it's:
They - now - cook - egg - for - breakfast.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:41.280
We can see that the sentence is SVO.

00:05:41.453 --> 00:05:43.999
As I said before, there
are no verb conjugations.

00:05:44.024 --> 00:05:46.440
So, how do we know that 
this action is happening now?

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:48.940
Well, it's because of this word 
right here: "sedang"

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:52.400
This is an adverb that indicates
that something is happening right now.

00:05:52.464 --> 00:05:55.232
You can change the tense of
the verb by changing this adverb.

00:05:55.257 --> 00:05:56.334
Here's another sentence:

00:05:58.671 --> 00:06:00.852
After changing the word
"sedang" to "sudah",

00:06:00.877 --> 00:06:03.618
the sentence now means:
"They cooked eggs for breakfast",

00:06:03.642 --> 00:06:04.663
in the past tense.

00:06:04.688 --> 00:06:07.305
The adverb "sudah" means
something like "already",

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:09.440
and shows that an action
has been completed.

00:06:09.552 --> 00:06:10.920
How about the future tense?

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:15.660
Here "akan" is the future adverb.

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:17.740
Another example:

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:23.700
This means:
"Like me, he often travels to Lombok".

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:28.219
Word-for-word, it's:
Like - I - he - often - (to) travel - to - Lombok

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:29.920
Another example:

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:33.360
This means : "Do you want to sit?"

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.460
"Apa" is a yes-or-no question marker.

00:06:36.485 --> 00:06:40.000
So word-for-word, it's : 
question - you -  want - sit

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:42.280
But "apa" is often left out in speech.

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:44.173
And notice that to express desire,

00:06:44.197 --> 00:06:46.860
you can just add "mau" right
before another verb.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:49.159
The second verb doesn't
change its form at all.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:50.387
Another example:

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:54.880
This means:
"I write letters for my friend"

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:58.820
Word-for-word, it's :
I - write - letter -  for - friend - I

00:06:58.924 --> 00:07:02.600
Notice that the word for "letters" :
"surat", is the same as the singular form.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:06.980
Also notice that possession is shown by placing
a personal pronoun right after a noun.

00:07:07.115 --> 00:07:10.323
"Teman saya" literally
"friend - I"  means "my friend".

00:07:10.347 --> 00:07:13.040
This makes it quite easy
to form possessives.

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:15.660
Another example:

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:19.760
This means "I don't like eating peanuts"

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:23.329
Word-for-word, it's:
I - no - like - eat - peanut.

00:07:23.354 --> 00:07:26.599
So, we negate a sentence by adding
"tidak" before the verb.

00:07:26.696 --> 00:07:29.775
And notice that similar to the
word "mau", which means "want",

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:33.804
we can express "like" by placing the
word "suka" right before another verb.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:38.700
This means:
"The peanuts have already been eaten"

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:41.553
Word by word, it's
"peanuts - already - be eaten',

00:07:41.577 --> 00:07:43.400
the verb "dimakan" is a passive.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:47.011
Let's take a look at some of the verbs
 from the above sentences.

00:07:47.036 --> 00:07:49.575
You can see that these Indonesian
verbs have two parts:

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:52.455
a root in black and an affix in red.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.620
Indonesian has many such affixes that are
added to roots to make verbs and nouns.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:59.820
One example, the root : "tulis",
meaning "to write".

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:00.480
We have

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:02.060
= "to write" (infinitive)

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:03.960
"to be written"

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:05.640
"to be written"

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:07.860
"something that's written"

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:10.240
"to write something down"

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:11.940
"a writer"

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:14.300
"the process of writing"

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:17.560
"to have (...) written on it"

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:20.860
"with the writing or words".

00:08:21.132 --> 00:08:24.337
You can see more examples of such
affixes in my video on Malay,

00:08:24.362 --> 00:08:25.819
which functions in the same way.

00:08:25.944 --> 00:08:28.490
Affixes are the most challenging
part of learning Indonesian,

00:08:28.515 --> 00:08:29.855
at least for a lot of learners.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:32.775
But, you can make use of that
feature of the roots and affixes

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:35.734
to help you learn new words through
their connections with other words.

00:08:35.759 --> 00:08:39.299
Standard Indonesian really is a simple
language, compared to other languages.

00:08:39.299 --> 00:08:40.965
But, that's Standard Indonesian,

00:08:40.989 --> 00:08:44.699
which is normally used as the written
language and in formal contexts.

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:46.625
The way Indonesian is spoken in daily life

00:08:46.649 --> 00:08:49.140
is often fairly different from
Standard Indonesian.

00:08:49.193 --> 00:08:52.518
Different varieties of colloquial
Indonesian are spoken around the country,

00:08:52.543 --> 00:08:55.915
and they're influenced by the local
native language in that area.

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:59.499
These different varieties of Indonesian
are referred to as "Bahasa Gaul".

00:08:59.586 --> 00:09:02.370
The Bahasa Gaul of Jakarta
is influenced by Betawi,

00:09:02.395 --> 00:09:04.939
the Malay creole language
that's native to Jakarta,

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:06.986
as well as by Javanese and Sundanese,

00:09:07.011 --> 00:09:09.660
since people from all over
Indonesia move to Jakarta.

00:09:09.916 --> 00:09:12.090
Let's look at the Bahasa Gaul of Jakarta,

00:09:12.115 --> 00:09:14.439
and see how it differs
from Standard Indonesian.

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:16.300
Here's a sentence we looked at before.

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:20.800
Now in Bahasa Gaul:

00:09:23.415 --> 00:09:25.645
The first difference we see
is that the word "sedang"

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:28.991
which indicates present tense
is "lagi" in Bahasa Gaul.

00:09:29.016 --> 00:09:32.720
"Lagi" is also used in Standard
Indonesian to mean "again" or "still",

00:09:32.745 --> 00:09:35.475
but it's not used as a present
tense marker like this.

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:37.357
Another difference is
that the verb "memasak"

00:09:37.382 --> 00:09:40.349
is used in its root form
"masak" without the prefix.

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:44.200
Then, we have a sound difference in the
word "telur", which becomes like "telor".

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:48.303
And then, "untuk" becomes "buat",
which can be used in Standard Indonesian,

00:09:48.328 --> 00:09:50.240
but the meaning is more like "in order to".

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:53.099
In Bahasa Gaul, you can use
it to simply mean "for".

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:57.380
Now, in Bahasa Gaul :

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.880
Here notice that the word
"sudah" is  reduced to "udah".

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:07.920
Now, in Bahasa Gaul :

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:13.600
Here notice that the future
marker "akan" becomes "bakal"

00:10:14.938 --> 00:10:15.960
Another example:

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:19.900
Now, in Bahasa Gaul :

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:24.566
The first difference here
is that the word "seperti",

00:10:24.591 --> 00:10:27.080
meaning "like", changes to "kayak".

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:31.080
Then, notice that "saya" becomes "gue",
which is a loanword from Betawi.

00:10:31.105 --> 00:10:33.921
And "berlibur" becomes
"liburan", the root is the same,

00:10:33.946 --> 00:10:35.326
but the affixes are different.

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:39.900
In this example, we'll see two
variations of Bahasa Gaul.

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:42.680
The first variation is a
simple casual sentence.

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:47.100
Notice that the yes-or-no question
marker "apa" isn't there.

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:50.860
Instead, at the end, you see "gak",
which means "no" in Bahasa Gaul.

00:10:51.243 --> 00:10:53.400
So it's like "You want to sit, no?"

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:55.772
The second variation
is used for close friends

00:10:55.797 --> 00:10:57.813
or people the same age as you or younger.

00:10:59.152 --> 00:11:00.983
The second person pronoun is "lu",

00:11:01.008 --> 00:11:03.230
which I believe is a loanword from Chinese,

00:11:03.255 --> 00:11:06.464
which is present in the Betawi
creole language of Jakarta.

00:11:07.143 --> 00:11:10.819
In this example, let's look at some options
for first-person pronouns.

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:23.020
In a casual-polite sentence,
you use "saya" even in Bahasa Gaul.

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:25.979
In a more casual familiar sentence,
"aku" is used.

00:11:26.004 --> 00:11:28.027
And when "aku" is
used as a possessive,

00:11:28.052 --> 00:11:30.451
a shortened form is attached
to the end of the noun.

00:11:30.508 --> 00:11:33.560
In a more intimate sentence,
"gua" or "gue" is used.

00:11:33.608 --> 00:11:36.183
Also notice that "menulis" loses its prefix,

00:11:36.208 --> 00:11:38.814
but it doesn't revert to
its root form "tulis",

00:11:38.839 --> 00:11:41.279
instead it maintains the nasal
sound at the beginning.

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:43.345
And, one more small difference we can see

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:46.775
is that the word for friend "teman"
is pronounced more like "temen".

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:48.980
And another example:

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:52.180
Now in Bahasa Gaul :

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:56.343
Again here we see the pronoun "gua",

00:11:56.367 --> 00:11:59.593
and we see that the negative
"tidak" becomes "nggak",

00:11:59.618 --> 00:12:02.820
which is related to the word
"gak" which we saw before.

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:06.400
And in Bahasa Gaul,
"suka" sometimes becomes "demen".

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:08.359
And one last example:

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:11.439
Now in Bahasa Gaul :

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:17.080
Here the only difference is that "sudah"
becomes "udah", as we saw before.

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:20.821
So, you can see that the
colloquial Indonesian of Jakarta

00:12:20.846 --> 00:12:23.180
is fairly different from
Standard indonesian.

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:25.859
And there are varieties like this
spoken around the country.

00:12:25.884 --> 00:12:29.668
The influence of the other languages
of Indonesia on colloquial Indonesian

00:12:29.693 --> 00:12:33.304
is one of the things that sets the language
apart from other varieties of Malay.

00:12:33.329 --> 00:12:35.973
Indonesian is definitely
a fun language to study,

00:12:35.998 --> 00:12:39.686
and communicating with Indonesians in
their national language and lingua franca

